WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.081
Good coordination starts with a clear intelligence requirement.

00:00:03.081 --> 00:00:07.552
A requirement states the decision to be supported, the audience, the timeframe,

00:00:07.552 --> 00:00:10.532
and the information needed to answer the question.

00:00:10.532 --> 00:00:14.258
It turns a broad topic into a focused analytic task.

00:00:14.258 --> 00:00:18.729
Without a clear requirement, teams can collect too much, share too widely,

00:00:18.729 --> 00:00:21.337
or confuse interesting information with necessary information.

00:00:21.337 --> 00:00:22.083
For example,

00:00:22.083 --> 00:00:26.926
a requirement might ask whether a supplier risk indicator affects currently used vendors,

00:00:26.926 --> 00:00:30.279
whether a fraud pattern is appearing across business units,

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:35.123
or whether a cyber threat report is relevant to the organization's exposed services.

00:00:35.123 --> 00:00:38.849
Each requirement implies a different audience, data need, and urgency.

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:42.947
A technical operations team may need actionable indicators and affected assets.

00:00:42.947 --> 00:00:46.301
Executives may need risk, impact, confidence, and decisions required.

00:00:46.301 --> 00:00:50.772
Legal or privacy reviewers may need source, authority, retention, and handling detail.

00:00:50.772 --> 00:00:54.498
Collection discipline means gathering only what is authorized, relevant, proportionate,

00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:56.360
and useful for the decision.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.577
It does not mean collecting every available log, every identity record, every partner note,

00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:04.185
or every personal identifier just in case.

00:01:04.185 --> 00:01:08.656
More data can increase privacy risk, make sharing harder, create retention burdens,

00:01:08.656 --> 00:01:10.146
and dilute analytic focus.

00:01:10.146 --> 00:01:15.362
The safest intelligence product often answers the decision question without exposing raw sensitive material.

00:01:15.362 --> 00:01:17.598
Minimization begins at the requirements stage.

00:01:17.598 --> 00:01:22.069
If the decision can be supported with aggregated counts, sanitized case summaries,

00:01:22.069 --> 00:01:24.677
pseudonymous identifiers, or a narrow time window,

00:01:24.677 --> 00:01:28.030
the team should not default to full raw datasets.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:32.129
If a partner only needs a trend, do not send names.

00:01:32.129 --> 00:01:37.345
If an operational team only needs affected systems, do not include unrelated personal details.

00:01:37.345 --> 00:01:41.071
If legal review is needed before using a data source,

00:01:41.071 --> 00:01:43.306
the requirement should name that condition.

00:01:43.306 --> 00:01:45.542
A strong requirement is also reviewable.

00:01:45.542 --> 00:01:50.013
It records assumptions, scope limits, data sources, expected output, caveats, and owner.

00:01:50.013 --> 00:01:51.503
If the question changes,

00:01:51.503 --> 00:01:55.229
the requirement should be updated rather than silently expanding collection.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:59.700
That discipline keeps intelligence work aligned with authority, privacy, and decision support.
